/-!
# A compact object with no finite graded-projective model

**[Stacks, Tag 09R5]**: there is a differential graded algebra `(A, d)` and a compact object `E`
of `D(A, d)` which cannot be represented by a differential graded `A`-module that is finite and
projective as a graded `A`-module. The algebra is `A = k[x, y]/(y²)` over `k = ZMod 2`, with `x`
of degree `0`, `y` of degree `-1` and `d y = x² + x`, and `E` is the direct summand of the regular
module cut out by multiplication by `x`, constructed in
`StacksFrontier/Examples/Algebra/DualNumberPolynomialIdempotent.lean`.

The obstruction is a parity count. A finite graded-projective `P` is projective as a plain right
`A`-module, so reducing it modulo the ideal generated by `x² - x` and `y` — that is, tensoring
with the augmentation `A → k[x]/(x² - x) = k × k` — gives a finite free `k × k`-module, whose
dimension over `k` is even. That reduction does not change homology, because the kernel of the
reduction is acyclic, and the homology of the total differential of `P` is its degree-zero
cohomology, since `P` represents `E` and `E` has cohomology only in degree zero. So the dimension
of `H⁰(P)` would be even, whereas `H⁰(E) = k` is one-dimensional.

## A mistake in the Stacks Project, pending review

The second half of this file is **not** a formalization of a tag. It is the machine-checked
refutation of the implication (1) ⟹ (2) of [Stacks, Tag 09RB], read literally. That implication
is **a mistake in the Stacks Project, pending review**: it is **contradicted by the source's own
[Stacks, Tag 09R5]**, whose compact object over the bounded algebra of §09R4 has no finite
graded-projective representative at all. No step of the refutation is left on paper.

What the source's proof does establish is registered under the tag, in
`StacksFrontier/Algebra/DifferentialGraded/Module/CompactFiniteFree.lean`:
`DifferentialGradedModule.exists_retract_isFiniteFreeAsGradedModule_of_isCompactObject` says that
a compact object is a **direct summand** in `D(A, d)` of the image of a module which is **finite
free** as a graded module and satisfies `Hom_{K(A, d)} = Hom_{D(A, d)}`, with no boundedness
hypothesis. The example below is consistent with that corrected form — its `E = Ker(x)` is a
direct summand of the regular module, which is finite free — and refutes only the literal
reading, with "represented by" in place of "direct summand" and "finite projective" in place of
"finite free". The converse implication (2) ⟹ (1) is true and is proved in
`StacksFrontier/Algebra/DifferentialGraded/Module/CompactFiniteProjective.lean`.

The source's own reasoning is followed. §09R4 says "it is clear that `A` is a compact object of
`D(A, d)`", which is
`DifferentialGradedModule.isCompactObject_q_regularModule_arbitraryUniverse`; it exhibits
`A = Ker(x) ⊕ Im(1 - x)` in `K(A, d)` and concludes "`Ker(x)` is a compact object as follows from
[Stacks, Tag 09QH]", which is
`DifferentialGradedModule.isCompactObject_of_retract_q_regularModule`; and the algebra above is
concentrated in degrees `0` and `-1`
(`Stacks.Examples.charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra_grading_eq_bot`), so it satisfies Tag 09RB's
hypothesis "`Aⁿ = 0` for `|n| ≫ 0`" with `c = 2`. Tag 09RB's (1) ⟹ (2), applied to that bounded
algebra and that compact object, contradicts Tag 09R5.

Two readings of the literal implication are refuted, with the same witness.

* `Stacks.Examples.not_forall_exists_finite_gradedProjective_of_isCompactObject` is (1) ⟹ (2)
  itself, with hypothesis (1) read as `IsCompactObject.{w}` for a universe `w` that is a parameter
  of the theorem. `IsCompactObject.{w}` tests the canonical map `⨁ᵢ Hom(E, Fᵢ) → Hom(E, ∐ᵢ Fᵢ)` on
  families indexed by `I : Type w`, whereas the source quantifies over arbitrary index sets; since
  `w` is arbitrary, the implication is refuted at *every* reading of that quantifier, and the
  witness is compact at all of them at once.
* `Stacks.Examples.not_forall_exists_finite_gradedProjective_of_retract_regularModule` is the
  specialisation of (1) ⟹ (2) whose hypothesis is "`E` is a retract of the image of the regular
  module", in which no compactness predicate occurs at all. Being a retract of `Q(A)` is a
  universe-free condition and implies compactness under every reading of the word, so this refutes
  a formally stronger statement than the first.

*The negated condition (2) is weaker than the source's, so the refutation is a fortiori.* Condition
(2) as negated below asks only for a `P` which is finite (`DifferentialGradedModule.IsFinite`,
Tag 09R1), is projective as a graded module in the sense of
`Stacks.DifferentialGraded.DGModule.IsProjectiveAsGradedModule` with its source modules confined
to `Type 0`, and whose image is isomorphic to `E` in `D(A, d)`. The source asks for all of that
*and* for `Hom_{K(A, d)}(P, M) = Hom_{D(A, d)}(P, M)` for every `M`, at no restriction on the
graded modules tested for the splitting. Dropping those two requirements makes (2) easier to
satisfy, hence makes (1) ⟹ (2) a weaker claim, hence makes its refutation stronger than what is
needed to contradict the tag.

*Where the source's error must lie.* The corrected form above is what 09RB's proof has in hand
once it has applied Proposition 22.36.4 ([Stacks, Tag 09R3]) and Lemma 22.22.3. The source does
not stop there: it goes on to build, by a cone construction, a finite graded free `P` representing
`E ⊕ E[n]`, and then splits `0 → P₁ ∩ P₂ → P₁ ⊕ P₂ → P → 0` to cut `E` out of `P` as an honest
representative — which, if it worked, would give the literal (2). That remainder is not formalized
anywhere in this library, and it is what the counterexample below shows cannot be right.

*Citations.* The refutations are
`Stacks.Examples.not_forall_exists_finite_gradedProjective_of_isCompactObject` (this file, line
1192) and `Stacks.Examples.not_forall_exists_finite_gradedProjective_of_retract_regularModule`
(this file, line 1123); both reduce to the obstruction
`Stacks.Examples.not_representable_by_finite_gradedProjective_of_retract_charTwoRegularModule`
(this file, line 964), which is also what proves Tag 09R5 itself at
`Stacks.Examples.exists_isCompactObject_not_representable_by_finite_gradedProjective` (this file,
line 1054). The compactness of the witness is
`Stacks.Examples.exists_isCompactObject_retract_charTwoRegularModule`
(`StacksFrontier/Examples/Algebra/DualNumberPolynomialIdempotent.lean:149`) together with
`DifferentialGradedModule.isCompactObject_of_retract_q_regularModule`
(`StacksFrontier/Algebra/DifferentialGraded/Module/RegularModuleCompactUniverse.lean:251`), and the
boundedness of the algebra is `Stacks.Examples.charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra_grading_eq_bot` (this
file, line 1070). The corrected equivalence is closed in both directions by
`DifferentialGradedModule.exists_retract_isFiniteFreeAsGradedModule_of_isCompactObject` and
`DifferentialGradedModule.isCompactObject_of_retract_isFiniteFreeAsGradedModule`, both in
`StacksFrontier/Algebra/DifferentialGraded/Module/CompactFiniteFree.lean`, whose module
documentation carries the same errata from the other side.

No step of the source's argument is left on paper; what is not formal, and cannot be, is only the
reading of Tag 09RB's sentences as the Lean statements below. In particular the universe caveat
under which the retract form was originally stated — that source compactness was known only to
*imply* `IsCompactObject.{0}`, so that refuting `IsCompactObject.{0} E → (2)` would have refuted
something formally stronger than the source's claim — is discharged by
`DifferentialGradedModule.isCompactObject_of_retract_q_regularModule`, which makes the witness
`IsCompactObject.{w}` for an arbitrary `w`, so that no reading of the quantifier on index sets
rescues the implication.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.Examples.homogeneousCover`: the free cover of a differential graded module by one
  shifted copy of the algebra per homogeneous element.
* `Stacks.Examples.charTwoReducedDifferential`: the differential of a differential graded
  `A`-module after reduction modulo the ideal generated by `x² - x` and `y`.
* `Stacks.Examples.totalHomologyEquivGradedCohomology`: for a module with cohomology only in
  degree zero, the homology of the total differential is the degree-zero cohomology.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Examples.moduleProjective_of_isProjectiveAsGradedModule`: a differential graded module
  projective as a graded module is projective as a module.
* `Stacks.Examples.even_finrank_homology_iff`: the homology of a square-zero endomorphism of a
  finite-dimensional space has even dimension exactly when the space does.
* `Stacks.Examples.cycle_in_relation_ideal_is_boundary` and
  `Stacks.Examples.homologyEquivOfSurjectiveWithAcyclicKernel`: the reduction does not change
  homology.
* `Stacks.Examples.even_finrank_totalHomology`: the total homology of a finite graded-projective
  differential graded `A`-module has even dimension.
* `Stacks.Examples.not_representable_by_finite_gradedProjective_of_retract_charTwoRegularModule`:
  the obstruction, stated with no compactness hypothesis — all it consumes is the retract data.
* `Stacks.Examples.exists_isCompactObject_not_representable_by_finite_gradedProjective`:
  **[Stacks, Tag 09R5]**.
* `Stacks.Examples.charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra_grading_eq_bot`: the algebra of Tag 09R4 vanishes
  in every degree `n` with `2 ≤ |n|`, so it satisfies the boundedness hypothesis of Tag 09RB.
* `Stacks.Examples.not_forall_exists_finite_gradedProjective_of_retract_regularModule`: over a
  bounded differential graded algebra, a retract of the image of the regular module need not be
  representable by a finite graded-projective module — **a refutation of the literal implication
  (1) ⟹ (2) of [Stacks, Tag 09RB], which is a mistake in the source, pending review; not a
  formalization of it**, carrying no Stacks tag.
* `Stacks.Examples.not_forall_exists_finite_gradedProjective_of_isCompactObject`: the same
  refutation with the source's own hypothesis, namely that `E` is a compact object, at an
  arbitrary index universe — **a refutation of the literal implication (1) ⟹ (2) of
  [Stacks, Tag 09RB], which is a mistake in the source, pending review; not a formalization of
  it**, carrying no Stacks tag.

## Implementation notes

*Graded projectivity.* Graded projectivity is taken in the form
`Stacks.DifferentialGraded.DGModule.IsProjectiveAsGradedModule`, the splitting of every
surjective graded homomorphism onto the module, which is how the converse implication
`DifferentialGradedModule.isCompactObject_of_exists_finite_projective_representative` of
Tag 09RB states it. No category of graded modules is needed: the splitting is applied once, to
the homogeneous free cover, and exhibits the module as a direct summand of a free module.

*Two spellings of the opposite algebra.* The right action of `A` on its modules is a module
structure over `A.Aᵐᵒᵖ`, whereas the ring-theoretic API used for the reduction speaks of
`(k[x][ε])ᵐᵒᵖ`. The two are the same ring, but instance search does not unfold the algebra far
enough to match the second against the first, so the action is recorded once in the second
spelling by `Stacks.Examples.moduleOppositeDualNumber`.

*Cohomology.* Cohomology of `D(A, d)` is `DifferentialGradedModule.cohomologyFunctor`, and
`DifferentialGradedModule.cohomologyFunctorIso` identifies it on a module with the explicit
cocycles-modulo-coboundaries description. Both pre-triangulated structures on `K(A, d)` are in
scope; the cone-triangle one is given priority, as in
`StacksFrontier/Algebra/DifferentialGraded/Module/DerivedCohomology.lean`, while the statement of the
tagged result is phrased in the primed derived category of
`StacksFrontier/Algebra/DifferentialGraded/Module/DerivedCategory.lean`, matching Tag 09RB.

*Universes.* The base ring, the algebra and its modules are all in `Type 0`, which is the shape in
which the characterisation of compact objects is available; the index universe of the families
testing compactness is *not* tied to that choice. Compactness of the regular module at an
arbitrary index universe is
`DifferentialGradedModule.isCompactObject_q_regularModule_arbitraryUniverse`, proved in
`StacksFrontier/Algebra/DifferentialGraded/Module/RegularModuleCompactUniverse.lean` from the fact
that a family of objects of `D(A, d)` admitting a direct sum has only `Type 0`-many nonzero
members, whatever its index type. That is what lets the refutation below quantify over the
reading of the source's index sets; see "A claim about the Stacks Project" above.

*The refutations deliberately carry no Stacks tag.* As recorded at length in
`StacksFrontier/RingTheory/DiscreteValuationRing/WeakSolutionObstruction.lean`, every registration
form in `StacksCore/Tactic/StacksAttribute.lean` — `@[stacks]`, `@[stacks_fixed]`, `add_to_stacks` —
ends in the same database entry, so none of them can express "this refutes the tag". The
registrations of 09RB in this repository stay where they are, on the two implications which are
true: `DifferentialGradedModule.isCompactObject_of_exists_finite_projective_representative` for
(2) ⟹ (1) (`StacksFrontier/Algebra/DifferentialGraded/Module/CompactFiniteProjective.lean:293`), and
`DifferentialGradedModule.exists_retract_isFiniteFreeAsGradedModule_of_isCompactObject` for the
corrected (1) ⟹ (2)
(`StacksFrontier/Algebra/DifferentialGraded/Module/CompactFiniteFree.lean:627`). Each records in
the note string of its attribute how the registered statement departs from the source's sentence;
that is the house pattern for a tag registered clause by clause, used for [Stacks, Tag 0AW0] in
Chapter 47 (`StacksFrontier/RingTheory/Dualizing/TorsionQuotient.lean:273` and the registrations
below it).

*Two spellings of the derived category.* `derivedCategory'` is the localization at
`quasiIsomorphisms'` and `derivedCategory` the localization at `quasiIsomorphisms`; the two
morphism properties have identical bodies and differ only in universe generality, so the types are
definitionally equal but not syntactically so. The obstruction lemma therefore takes its object in
the *unprimed* category, which is the one `cohomologyFunctor` consumes in its proof, while the
statements quantified over a general algebra use the primed spelling, which is the one Tag 09RB
and Tag 09R5 are phrased in. Declaring the obstruction's object in the primed category instead
makes `cohomologyFunctor` ill-typed at `implicit` transparency.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 09R5](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09R5)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 09R4](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09R4), the example.
* [Stacks Project, Tag 09RB](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09RB), whose condition (2) is
  the one negated here, and whose implication (1) ⟹ (2) is refuted below.
* [Stacks Project, Tag 09QH](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09QH), the source's step that a
  direct summand of a compact object is compact.
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits DifferentialGradedModule
open scoped DirectSum TensorProduct

namespace Stacks.Examples

universe w u

/-! ### Graded projectivity gives projectivity of the underlying module -/

/-- Classical decidable equality, a local instance used to form the direct sum indexed by the
homogeneous elements of a differential graded module. -/
@[instance_reducible]
def classicalDecEq (α : Type*) : DecidableEq α := Classical.typeDecidableEq α

attribute [local instance] classicalDecEq

section GradedProjective

variable {R : Type u} [CommRing R] {A : DifferentialGradedAlgebra.{u, u} R}

/-- Right multiplication on a fixed element of a differential graded module, as an `R`-linear map
out of the algebra. -/
def rightMulLinear (M : DifferentialGradedModule.{u, u, u} A) (x : M.M) : A.A →ₗ[R] M.M where
  toFun a := MulOpposite.op a • x
  map_add' a b := by simp only [MulOpposite.op_add, add_smul]
  map_smul' r a := by simp only [MulOpposite.op_smul, smul_assoc, RingHom.id_apply]

/-- The homogeneous free cover of a differential graded module `M`: the direct sum of one copy of
the algebra for every homogeneous element of `M`, mapping the generator indexed by `x` to `x`.
Shifting the `x`-th summand into degree `-deg x` makes it a graded homomorphism out of
`DifferentialGradedModule.directSumShifts`. -/
def homogeneousCover (M : DifferentialGradedModule.{u, u, u} A) :
    (⨁ _ : (Σ n : ℤ, M.grading n), A.A) →ₗ[R] M.M :=
  DirectSum.toModule R (Σ n : ℤ, M.grading n) M.M fun i => rightMulLinear M i.2

/-- The homogeneous free cover sends the `a`-multiple of the generator indexed by `x` to `x a`. -/
theorem homogeneousCover_of (M : DifferentialGradedModule.{u, u, u} A)
    (i : Σ n : ℤ, M.grading n) (a : A.A) :
    homogeneousCover M (DirectSum.of (fun _ => A.A) i a) = MulOpposite.op a • (i.2 : M.M) :=
  DirectSum.toModule_lof R (M := fun _ : Σ n : ℤ, M.grading n => A.A) (N := M.M) i a

/-- The homogeneous free cover is surjective: every homogeneous element is the image of its own
generator. -/
theorem homogeneousCover_surjective (M : DifferentialGradedModule.{u, u, u} A) :
    Function.Surjective (homogeneousCover M) := by
  intro x
  induction x using DirectSum.Decomposition.inductionOn M.grading with
  | zero => exact ⟨0, map_zero _⟩
  | add x y hx hy =>
    obtain ⟨a, rfl⟩ := hx
    obtain ⟨b, rfl⟩ := hy
    exact ⟨a + b, map_add _ _ _⟩
  | @homogeneous n x =>
    refine ⟨DirectSum.of (fun _ => A.A) ⟨n, x⟩ 1, ?_⟩
    rw [homogeneousCover_of]
    simp

/-- The homogeneous free cover is right `A`-linear. -/
theorem homogeneousCover_smul (M : DifferentialGradedModule.{u, u, u} A) (a : A.A)
    (x : ⨁ _ : (Σ n : ℤ, M.grading n), A.A) :
    homogeneousCover M (MulOpposite.op a • x) =
      MulOpposite.op a • homogeneousCover M x := by
  induction x using DirectSum.induction_on with
  | zero => simp
  | add x y hx hy => simp only [smul_add, map_add, hx, hy]
  | of i b =>
    rw [← DirectSum.of_smul, homogeneousCover_of, homogeneousCover_of]
    change MulOpposite.op (b * a) • (i.2 : M.M) =
      MulOpposite.op a • (MulOpposite.op b • (i.2 : M.M))
    rw [MulOpposite.op_mul, mul_smul]

/-- The homogeneous free cover preserves the grading: the generator indexed by a homogeneous
element of degree `d` sits in degree `d`, so the summands must be shifted by `-d`. -/
theorem homogeneousCover_mem (M : DifferentialGradedModule.{u, u, u} A) (n : ℤ)
    (x : ⨁ _ : (Σ n : ℤ, M.grading n), A.A)
    (hx : ∀ i : Σ n : ℤ, M.grading n, x i ∈ A.grading (n + -i.1)) :
    homogeneousCover M x ∈ M.grading n := by
  rw [← DirectSum.sum_support_of x, map_sum]
  refine Submodule.sum_mem _ fun i _ => ?_
  rw [homogeneousCover_of]
  simpa only [show i.1 + (n + -i.1) = n by omega] using
    M.smul_mem i.1 (n + -i.1) i.2 (x i) i.2.property (hx i)

/-- A ring is free as a module over its opposite. -/
theorem free_opposite_self (S : Type*) [Ring S] : Module.Free (MulOpposite S) S := by
  let e : S ≃ₗ[MulOpposite S] MulOpposite S :=
    { __ := MulOpposite.opAddEquiv
      map_smul' := by intros; rfl }
  exact Module.Free.of_equiv e.symm

/-- **A differential graded module that is projective as a graded module is projective as a
module.** Its homogeneous free cover is a graded surjection onto it from a free module, so graded
projectivity splits that cover, exhibiting the module as a direct summand of a free module. -/
theorem moduleProjective_of_isProjectiveAsGradedModule (P : DifferentialGradedModule.{u, u, u} A)
    (hP : Stacks.DifferentialGraded.DGModule.IsProjectiveAsGradedModule.{u, u, u, u}
      (Unbundled.module P)) :
    Module.Projective (MulOpposite A.A) P.M := by
  let F := directSumShifts A fun i : Σ n : ℤ, P.grading n => -i.1
  let cover : F.M →ₗ[MulOpposite A.A] P.M :=
    { __ := (homogeneousCover P).toAddHom
      map_smul' := fun a x => homogeneousCover_smul P a.unop x }
  obtain ⟨s, hs⟩ := hP.2 (Unbundled.module F)
    ⟨(Unbundled.module_isLawful F).isInternal, (Unbundled.module_isLawful F).grading_smul⟩
    ⟨cover, fun n x hx => homogeneousCover_mem P n x hx⟩ (homogeneousCover_surjective P)
  have : Module.Free (MulOpposite A.A) A.A := free_opposite_self A.A
  have : Module.Free (MulOpposite A.A) F.M :=
    inferInstanceAs (Module.Free (MulOpposite A.A)
      (⨁ _ : (Σ n : ℤ, P.grading n), A.A))
  exact Module.Projective.of_split s.toFun cover (LinearMap.ext hs)

end GradedProjective

/-! ### The parity of the dimension of homology -/

section Parity

/-- For a square-zero endomorphism of a finite-dimensional space, the dimension of the homology
and the dimension of the space differ by twice the rank. -/
theorem finrank_homology_add_twice_finrank_range {K V : Type*} [DivisionRing K] [AddCommGroup V]
    [Module K V] [FiniteDimensional K V] (d : V →ₗ[K] V) (hd : d ∘ₗ d = 0) :
    Module.finrank K
          (LinearMap.ker d ⧸ (LinearMap.range d).comap (LinearMap.ker d).subtype) +
        2 * Module.finrank K (LinearMap.range d) = Module.finrank K V := by
  have h : LinearMap.range d ≤ LinearMap.ker d := by
    rintro _ ⟨x, rfl⟩
    exact LinearMap.congr_fun hd x
  have h₁ := Submodule.finrank_quotient_add_finrank
    ((LinearMap.range d).comap (LinearMap.ker d).subtype)
  have h₂ := d.finrank_range_add_finrank_ker
  have h₃ := (Submodule.comapSubtypeEquivOfLe h).finrank_eq
  omega

/-- The homology of a square-zero endomorphism of a finite-dimensional space has even dimension
exactly when the space does. -/
theorem even_finrank_homology_iff {K V : Type*} [DivisionRing K] [AddCommGroup V] [Module K V]
    [FiniteDimensional K V] (d : V →ₗ[K] V) (hd : d ∘ₗ d = 0) :
    Even (Module.finrank K
        (LinearMap.ker d ⧸ (LinearMap.range d).comap (LinearMap.ker d).subtype)) ↔
      Even (Module.finrank K V) := by
  rw [← finrank_homology_add_twice_finrank_range d hd, Nat.even_add]
  simp

/-- A finite projective module over a ring that maps to a principal ideal domain `S`, which in
turn maps to `T`, becomes free after base change to `T`: it is already free after base change to
`S`, because a finite projective module over a principal ideal domain is free. -/
@[sga "6 IX 1.7" "more general"]
theorem free_tensorProduct_of_pid_factor (R S T M : Type*) [CommRing R] [CommRing S]
    [IsPrincipalIdealRing S] [IsDomain S] [CommRing T] [Algebra R S] [Algebra R T] [Algebra S T]
    [IsScalarTower R S T] [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] [Module.Finite R M]
    [Module.Projective R M] : Module.Free T (T ⊗[R] M) := by
  have : Module.Projective S (S ⊗[R] M) := inferInstance
  have : Module.Finite S (S ⊗[R] M) := inferInstance
  have : Module.Free S (S ⊗[R] M) := inferInstance
  exact Module.Free.of_equiv (TensorProduct.AlgebraTensorModule.cancelBaseChange R S T T M)

/-- Base changing a finite projective module to `K × K` through a principal ideal domain gives a
free `K × K`-module, whose dimension over `K` is therefore even. -/
theorem even_finrank_tensorProduct_of_pid_factor (K R S M : Type*) [Field K] [CommRing R]
    [CommRing S] [IsPrincipalIdealRing S] [IsDomain S] [Algebra R S] [Algebra R (K × K)]
    [Algebra S (K × K)] [IsScalarTower R S (K × K)] [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M]
    [Module.Finite R M] [Module.Projective R M] :
    Even (Module.finrank K ((K × K) ⊗[R] M)) := by
  have : Module.Free (K × K) ((K × K) ⊗[R] M) := free_tensorProduct_of_pid_factor R S (K × K) M
  rw [← Module.finrank_mul_finrank K (K × K) ((K × K) ⊗[R] M)]
  simp [Module.finrank_prod]

/-- On a projective module, an element killed by a ring element `a` whose annihilator in the ring
is contained in the image of multiplication by `a` is itself `a` times something. -/
theorem exists_smul_eq_of_projective {T M : Type*} [Semiring T] [AddCommMonoid M] [Module T M]
    [Module.Projective T M] (a : T) (ha : ∀ b : T, a * b = 0 → ∃ c : T, a * c = b) (x : M)
    (hx : a • x = 0) : ∃ y : M, a • y = x := by
  classical
  obtain ⟨s, hs⟩ := (Module.projective_def (R := T) (P := M)).mp inferInstance
  have h : a • s x = 0 := by rw [← map_smul, hx, map_zero]
  have h' (i : M) : a * s x i = 0 := congrArg (fun t : M →₀ T => t i) h
  choose c hc using fun i : M => ha (s x i) (h' i)
  refine ⟨∑ i ∈ (s x).support, c i • i, ?_⟩
  rw [Finset.smul_sum]
  calc
    ∑ i ∈ (s x).support, a • (c i • i) = ∑ i ∈ (s x).support, s x i • i := by
      refine Finset.sum_congr rfl fun i _ => ?_
      rw [smul_smul, hc]
    _ = x := hs x

/-- On a projective module, multiplication by a nonzerodivisor of the ring is injective. -/
theorem smul_injective_of_projective {T M : Type*} [Ring T] [AddCommGroup M] [Module T M]
    [Module.Projective T M] (a : T) (ha : Function.Injective fun b : T => a * b) :
    Function.Injective fun x : M => a • x := by
  obtain ⟨s, hs⟩ := (Module.projective_def (R := T) (P := M)).mp inferInstance
  intro x y hxy
  refine hs.injective (Finsupp.ext fun i => ha ?_)
  simpa only [map_smul, Finsupp.smul_apply, smul_eq_mul] using
    congrArg (fun t : M →₀ T => t i) (congrArg s hxy)

/-- On a projective module over the opposite of the dual numbers, multiplication by `p` for a
nonzerodivisor `p` of the base is injective. -/
theorem inl_smul_injective_of_projective {S M : Type*} [CommRing S] [AddCommGroup M]
    [Module (MulOpposite (DualNumber S)) M] [Module.Projective (MulOpposite (DualNumber S)) M]
    (p : S) (hp : Function.Injective fun s : S => p * s) :
    Function.Injective fun x : M =>
      MulOpposite.op (TrivSqZeroExt.inl p : DualNumber S) • x := by
  refine smul_injective_of_projective _ fun a b hab => ?_
  refine MulOpposite.unop_injective (TrivSqZeroExt.ext (hp ?_) (hp ?_))
  · simpa only [MulOpposite.unop_mul, MulOpposite.unop_op, TrivSqZeroExt.fst_mul,
      TrivSqZeroExt.fst_inl, mul_comm] using
      congrArg (fun t : MulOpposite (DualNumber S) => t.unop.fst) hab
  · simpa only [MulOpposite.unop_mul, MulOpposite.unop_op, DualNumber.snd_mul,
      TrivSqZeroExt.fst_inl, TrivSqZeroExt.snd_inl, mul_zero, zero_mul, zero_add, add_zero,
      mul_comm] using
      congrArg (fun t : MulOpposite (DualNumber S) => t.unop.snd) hab

end Parity

/-! ### Reduction modulo the ideal generated by `x² - x` and `y` -/

/-- The right action of `A = k[x, y]/(y²)` on a differential graded `A`-module, in the spelling
`(k[x][ε])ᵐᵒᵖ` rather than `A.Aᵐᵒᵖ`. The two are the same ring, but instance search does not
unfold the algebra far enough to match the second spelling against the first, so the action is
recorded once in the spelling that the ring-theoretic API uses. -/
instance moduleOppositeDualNumber
    (P : DifferentialGradedModule.{0, 0, 0} charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra) :
    Module (MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))) P.M := P.rmodule

/-- `k × k` as an algebra over `A^op`, through the augmentation of the algebra of Tag 09R4. -/
@[instance_reducible]
def charTwoAugmentationAlgebra :
    Algebra (MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))) (ZMod 2 × ZMod 2) :=
  ((dualNumberAugmentation (ZMod 2)).toRingHom.fromOpposite
    (fun _ _ => Commute.all _ _)).toAlgebra

/-- `k[x]` as an algebra over `A^op`, through the projection killing `y`. -/
@[instance_reducible]
def charTwoDegreeZeroAlgebra :
    Algebra (MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))) (Polynomial (ZMod 2)) :=
  ((TrivSqZeroExt.fstHom (ZMod 2) (Polynomial (ZMod 2))
    (Polynomial (ZMod 2))).toRingHom.fromOpposite (fun _ _ => Commute.all _ _)).toAlgebra

/-- `k × k` as an algebra over `k[x]`, through evaluation at the two roots of `x² - x`. -/
@[instance_reducible]
def charTwoEvalAlgebra : Algebra (Polynomial (ZMod 2)) (ZMod 2 × ZMod 2) :=
  (polynomialEvalZeroOne (ZMod 2)).toRingHom.toAlgebra

attribute [local instance] charTwoAugmentationAlgebra charTwoDegreeZeroAlgebra charTwoEvalAlgebra

/-- The augmentation `A^op → k × k` factors through `k[x]`. -/
instance isScalarTower_charTwoAugmentation :
    IsScalarTower (MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))) (Polynomial (ZMod 2))
      (ZMod 2 × ZMod 2) :=
  IsScalarTower.of_algebraMap_eq fun _ => rfl

/-- **The reduction of a finite projective module modulo `(x² - x, y)` has even dimension**: it
is free over `k × k`, because `A^op` maps to `k × k` through the principal ideal domain `k[x]`. -/
theorem even_finrank_charTwoReduction (M : Type) [AddCommGroup M]
    [Module (MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))) M]
    [Module.Finite (MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))) M]
    [Module.Projective (MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))) M] :
    Even (Module.finrank (ZMod 2)
      ((ZMod 2 × ZMod 2) ⊗[MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))] M)) :=
  even_finrank_tensorProduct_of_pid_factor (ZMod 2)
    (MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))) (Polynomial (ZMod 2)) M

/-- Multiplication by `x² - x` is injective on a projective module over `A^op`. -/
theorem relation_smul_injective (M : Type) [AddCommGroup M]
    [Module (MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))) M]
    [Module.Projective (MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))) M] :
    Function.Injective fun x : M =>
      MulOpposite.op (TrivSqZeroExt.inl (Polynomial.X ^ 2 - Polynomial.X) :
        DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2))) • x :=
  inl_smul_injective_of_projective _ fun _ _ h =>
    mul_left_cancel₀ (monic_X_sq_sub_X (ZMod 2)).ne_zero h

/-- Tensoring `a • m` with `1` is the same as tensoring `m` with the image of `a`. -/
private theorem one_tmul_smul_eq_algebraMap_tmul {R B M : Type*} [CommRing R] [CommRing B]
    [Algebra R B] [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] (a : R) (m : M) :
    (1 : B) ⊗ₜ[R] (a • m) = algebraMap R B a ⊗ₜ[R] m := by
  rw [TensorProduct.tmul_smul, TensorProduct.smul_tmul', Algebra.smul_def, mul_one]

/-- The base change of a differential along an algebra map killing the twisting term `δ`: the
Leibniz rule `d (a x) = a (d x) + δ(a) x` makes `1 ⊗ d` well defined over `B` as soon as `δ`
lands in the kernel of `R → B`. -/
def baseChangeDifferential {R B M : Type*} [CommRing R] [CommRing B] [Algebra R B]
    [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] (d : M →+ M) (δ : R → R)
    (hδ : ∀ a, algebraMap R B (δ a) = 0) (hd : ∀ a x, d (a • x) = a • d x + δ a • x) :
    (B ⊗[R] M) →ₗ[B] (B ⊗[R] M) :=
  LinearMap.liftBaseChange B
    { toFun := fun x => (1 : B) ⊗ₜ[R] d x
      map_add' x y := by rw [map_add, TensorProduct.tmul_add]
      map_smul' a x := by
        change (1 : B) ⊗ₜ[R] d (a • x) = a • ((1 : B) ⊗ₜ[R] d x)
        rw [hd, TensorProduct.tmul_add, TensorProduct.tmul_smul, TensorProduct.tmul_smul]
        have h : δ a • ((1 : B) ⊗ₜ[R] x) = 0 := by
          rw [TensorProduct.smul_tmul', Algebra.smul_def, hδ, zero_mul,
            TensorProduct.zero_tmul]
        rw [h, add_zero] }

/-- The base change of `d` sends `b ⊗ₜ x` to `b ⊗ₜ d x`. -/
@[simp] theorem baseChangeDifferential_tmul {R B M : Type*} [CommRing R] [CommRing B]
    [Algebra R B] [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] (d : M →+ M) (δ : R → R)
    (hδ : ∀ a, algebraMap R B (δ a) = 0) (hd : ∀ a x, d (a • x) = a • d x + δ a • x) (b : B)
    (x : M) : baseChangeDifferential d δ hδ hd (b ⊗ₜ[R] x) = b ⊗ₜ[R] d x := by
  rw [baseChangeDifferential, LinearMap.liftBaseChange_tmul]
  change (b * 1) ⊗ₜ[R] d x = b ⊗ₜ[R] d x
  rw [mul_one]

/-- The base change of a square-zero differential squares to zero. -/
theorem baseChangeDifferential_comp_self {R B M : Type*} [CommRing R] [CommRing B] [Algebra R B]
    [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] (d : M →+ M) (δ : R → R)
    (hδ : ∀ a, algebraMap R B (δ a) = 0) (hd : ∀ a x, d (a • x) = a • d x + δ a • x)
    (hdd : ∀ x, d (d x) = 0) :
    baseChangeDifferential d δ hδ hd ∘ₗ baseChangeDifferential d δ hδ hd = 0 := by
  refine LinearMap.ext fun z => ?_
  change baseChangeDifferential d δ hδ hd (baseChangeDifferential d δ hδ hd z) = 0
  induction z using TensorProduct.induction_on with
  | zero => simp only [map_zero]
  | add x y hx hy => simp only [map_add, hx, hy, add_zero]
  | tmul b x => rw [baseChangeDifferential_tmul, baseChangeDifferential_tmul, hdd,
      TensorProduct.tmul_zero]

/-- In characteristic two the sign `(-1)^n` acts trivially. -/
theorem negOnePow_smul_charTwo {M : Type*} [AddCommGroup M] [Module (ZMod 2) M] (n : ℤ) (x : M) :
    (n.negOnePow : ℤ) • x = x := by
  rw [← Int.cast_smul_eq_zsmul (ZMod 2), Int.coe_negOnePow]
  simp only [show (-1 : ZMod 2) = 1 by decide, one_pow, one_smul]

/-- The Leibniz rule for the algebra of Tag 09R4, with no sign and no homogeneity assumption:
the sign is trivial in characteristic two, and both sides are additive in the element. -/
theorem charTwo_leibniz (P : DifferentialGradedModule.{0, 0, 0} charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra)
    (a : DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2))) (x : P.M) :
    P.d (MulOpposite.op a • x) =
      MulOpposite.op a • P.d x + MulOpposite.op (charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra.d a) • x := by
  induction x using DirectSum.Decomposition.inductionOn P.grading with
  | zero => simp only [smul_zero, map_zero, add_zero]
  | add x y hx hy =>
    simp only [smul_add, map_add, hx, hy]
    abel
  | @homogeneous n x => rw [P.leibniz_apply n x x.property a, negOnePow_smul_charTwo]

/-- The differential of a differential graded module over the algebra of Tag 09R4, reduced
modulo the ideal generated by `x² - x` and `y`: the Leibniz rule is unobstructed after reduction
because `d` of the algebra lands in that ideal. -/
def charTwoReducedDifferential
    (P : DifferentialGradedModule.{0, 0, 0} charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra) :
    ((ZMod 2 × ZMod 2) ⊗[MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))] P.M) →ₗ[ZMod 2 × ZMod 2]
      ((ZMod 2 × ZMod 2) ⊗[MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))] P.M) :=
  baseChangeDifferential P.d.toAddMonoidHom
    (fun a => MulOpposite.op (charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra.d a.unop))
    (fun a => dualNumberAugmentation_differential a.unop)
    (fun a x => by
      have h := charTwo_leibniz P a.unop x
      rw [MulOpposite.op_unop] at h
      exact h)

/-- The reduced differential sends `b ⊗ₜ x` to `b ⊗ₜ d x`. -/
@[simp] theorem charTwoReducedDifferential_tmul
    (P : DifferentialGradedModule.{0, 0, 0} charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra) (b : ZMod 2 × ZMod 2)
    (x : P.M) :
    charTwoReducedDifferential P
        (b ⊗ₜ[MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))] x) =
      b ⊗ₜ[MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))] P.d x :=
  baseChangeDifferential_tmul _ _ _ _ b x

/-- The reduced differential squares to zero. -/
theorem charTwoReducedDifferential_comp_self
    (P : DifferentialGradedModule.{0, 0, 0} charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra) :
    charTwoReducedDifferential P ∘ₗ charTwoReducedDifferential P = 0 :=
  baseChangeDifferential_comp_self _ _ _ _ fun x => LinearMap.congr_fun P.d_comp_d x

/-! ### Comparing the homology of a module with that of its reduction -/

section Reduction

/-- Tensoring with the unit is surjective when the structure map is. -/
theorem tensor_one_surjective_of_surjective {R B M : Type*} [CommRing R] [CommRing B]
    [Algebra R B] [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] (h : Function.Surjective (algebraMap R B)) :
    Function.Surjective fun x : M => (1 : B) ⊗ₜ[R] x := by
  intro z
  induction z using TensorProduct.induction_on with
  | zero => exact ⟨0, by simp⟩
  | tmul b m =>
    obtain ⟨a, rfl⟩ := h b
    exact ⟨a • m, one_tmul_smul_eq_algebraMap_tmul a m⟩
  | add x y hx hy =>
    obtain ⟨a, rfl⟩ := hx
    obtain ⟨b, rfl⟩ := hy
    exact ⟨a + b, TensorProduct.tmul_add _ _ _⟩

/-- An element of a projective module whose image in `B ⊗ M` vanishes lies in the submodule
generated by the two elements `p` and `y` of the kernel of `R → B`, provided those two generate
that kernel. -/
theorem tensor_one_eq_zero_of_projective {R B M : Type*} [CommRing R] [CommRing B] [Algebra R B]
    [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] [Module.Projective R M] (p y : R)
    (hker : ∀ a : R, algebraMap R B a = 0 → ∃ b c : R, a = p * b + y * c) (m : M)
    (hm : (1 : B) ⊗ₜ[R] m = 0) : ∃ a b : M, m = p • a + y • b := by
  classical
  obtain ⟨s, hs⟩ := (Module.projective_def (R := R) (P := M)).mp inferInstance
  have hcoeff (i : M) : algebraMap R B (s m i) = 0 := by
    have h := congrArg ((TensorProduct.rid R B).toLinearMap.comp
      (TensorProduct.map LinearMap.id ((Finsupp.lapply i).comp s))) hm
    simpa [Algebra.smul_def] using h
  choose a b hab using fun i : M => hker (s m i) (hcoeff i)
  refine ⟨∑ i ∈ (s m).support, a i • i, ∑ i ∈ (s m).support, b i • i, ?_⟩
  rw [Finset.smul_sum, Finset.smul_sum, ← Finset.sum_add_distrib]
  calc
    m = ∑ i ∈ (s m).support, s m i • i := (hs m).symm
    _ = _ := by
      refine Finset.sum_congr rfl fun i _ => ?_
      rw [hab i, add_smul, mul_smul, mul_smul]

/-- **A cocycle lying in the ideal generated by `p` and `y` is a coboundary**, when `y` squares to
zero, `d y + y d = p` acts injectively, and `f` kills the image of `y`. This is the two-step
argument of Tag 09R5 — reduce modulo `p`, then modulo `y` — carried out in one go. -/
theorem cycle_in_relation_ideal_is_boundary {K M V : Type*} [CommRing K] [AddCommGroup M]
    [Module K M] [AddCommGroup V] [Module K V] (d p y : M →ₗ[K] M) (f : M →ₗ[K] V)
    (hd : d ∘ₗ d = 0) (hy : y ∘ₗ y = 0) (hleib : ∀ x, d (y x) + y (d x) = p x)
    (hp : Function.Injective p) (hfy : ∀ x, f (y x) = 0)
    (hker : ∀ x, f x = 0 → ∃ a b, x = p a + y b) (x : M) (hfx : f x = 0) (hdx : d x = 0) :
    ∃ z : M, f z = 0 ∧ d z = x := by
  obtain ⟨a, b, hab⟩ := hker x hfx
  have heq : x = d (y a) + y (d a + b) := by
    rw [hab, ← hleib a, map_add]
    abel
  have hz : d (y (d a + b)) = 0 := by
    rwa [heq, map_add, show d (d (y a)) = 0 from LinearMap.congr_fun hd (y a), zero_add] at hdx
  have hzero : y (d a + b) = 0 := by
    refine hp ?_
    rw [map_zero, ← hleib, show y (y (d a + b)) = 0 from LinearMap.congr_fun hy (d a + b),
      map_zero, hz, map_zero, add_zero]
  exact ⟨y a, hfy a, by simpa [hzero] using heq.symm⟩

/-- **The homology of a differential is computed by its image under a surjection whose kernel is
acyclic.** -/
def homologyEquivOfSurjectiveWithAcyclicKernel {K M N : Type*} [CommRing K] [AddCommGroup M]
    [Module K M] [AddCommGroup N] [Module K N] (d : M →ₗ[K] M) (e : N →ₗ[K] N) (f : M →ₗ[K] N)
    (hd : d ∘ₗ d = 0) (hf : ∀ x, f (d x) = e (f x)) (hs : Function.Surjective f)
    (ha : ∀ x, f x = 0 → d x = 0 → ∃ z, f z = 0 ∧ d z = x) :
    (LinearMap.ker d ⧸ (LinearMap.range d).comap (LinearMap.ker d).subtype) ≃ₗ[K]
      (LinearMap.ker e ⧸ (LinearMap.range e).comap (LinearMap.ker e).subtype) := by
  have hd_apply (z : M) : d (d z) = 0 := LinearMap.congr_fun hd z
  let B := (LinearMap.range d).comap (LinearMap.ker d).subtype
  let C := (LinearMap.range e).comap (LinearMap.ker e).subtype
  let g : LinearMap.ker d →ₗ[K] LinearMap.ker e :=
    (f.comp (LinearMap.ker d).subtype).codRestrict (LinearMap.ker e) fun x => by
      change e (f x) = 0
      rw [← hf, x.property, map_zero]
  let l : LinearMap.ker d →ₗ[K] (LinearMap.ker e ⧸ C) := C.mkQ.comp g
  have hl : B ≤ LinearMap.ker l := by
    rintro x ⟨z, hz⟩
    change C.mkQ (g x) = 0
    refine (Submodule.Quotient.mk_eq_zero C).mpr ⟨f z, ?_⟩
    rw [← hf, hz]
    rfl
  refine LinearEquiv.ofBijective (B.liftQ l hl) ⟨?_, ?_⟩
  · refine (LinearMap.ker_eq_bot).mp ((Submodule.eq_bot_iff _).mpr fun x hx => ?_)
    induction x using Submodule.Quotient.induction_on with
    | H x =>
      change C.mkQ (g x) = 0 at hx
      obtain ⟨b, hb⟩ := (Submodule.Quotient.mk_eq_zero C).mp hx
      change e b = f x.val at hb
      obtain ⟨a, rfl⟩ := hs b
      have hx' : f (x.val - d a) = 0 := by rw [map_sub, hf, hb, sub_self]
      have hd' : d (x.val - d a) = 0 := by rw [map_sub, x.property, hd_apply, sub_self]
      obtain ⟨z, _, hz⟩ := ha _ hx' hd'
      refine (Submodule.Quotient.mk_eq_zero B).mpr ⟨z + a, ?_⟩
      change d (z + a) = x.val
      rw [map_add, hz, sub_add_cancel]
  · intro x
    induction x using Submodule.Quotient.induction_on with
    | H x =>
      obtain ⟨a, ha'⟩ := hs x.val
      have hfa : f (d a) = 0 := by
        rw [hf, ha']
        exact x.property
      obtain ⟨b, hfb, hdb⟩ := ha (d a) hfa (hd_apply a)
      have hcycle : d (a - b) = 0 := by rw [map_sub, hdb, sub_self]
      refine ⟨B.mkQ ⟨a - b, hcycle⟩, ?_⟩
      change C.mkQ (g ⟨a - b, hcycle⟩) = C.mkQ x
      congr 1
      refine Subtype.ext ?_
      change f (a - b) = x.val
      rw [map_sub, ha', hfb, sub_zero]

/-- The Leibniz rule for `ε`: `d (ε z) + ε (d z) = (x² - x) z`, as `2 = 0`. -/
theorem charTwo_d_eps_smul_add_eps_smul_d
    (P : DifferentialGradedModule.{0, 0, 0} charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra) (z : P.M) :
    P.d (MulOpposite.op (DualNumber.eps : DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2))) • z) +
        MulOpposite.op (DualNumber.eps : DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2))) • P.d z =
      MulOpposite.op (TrivSqZeroExt.inl (Polynomial.X ^ 2 - Polynomial.X) :
        DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2))) • z := by
  have heps : charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra.d DualNumber.eps =
      TrivSqZeroExt.inl (Polynomial.X ^ 2 - Polynomial.X) := by
    refine TrivSqZeroExt.ext ?_ ?_ <;> simp
  have htwo (a : P.M) : a + a = 0 := by
    rw [← two_smul (ZMod 2) a, show (2 : ZMod 2) = 0 from rfl, zero_smul]
  rw [charTwo_leibniz, heps]
  change _ + MulOpposite.op (TrivSqZeroExt.inl (Polynomial.X ^ 2 - Polynomial.X) :
    DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2))) • z + _ = _
  rw [add_right_comm, htwo, zero_add]

/-- Reducing modulo the augmentation kills `ε`-multiples: `1 ⊗ₜ (ε z) = 0`. -/
theorem tensor_one_eps_smul_eq_zero (M : Type) [AddCommGroup M]
    [Module (MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))) M] (z : M) :
    (1 : ZMod 2 × ZMod 2) ⊗ₜ[MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))]
      (MulOpposite.op (DualNumber.eps : DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2))) • z) = 0 := by
  have hyφ : algebraMap (MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))) (ZMod 2 × ZMod 2)
      (MulOpposite.op DualNumber.eps) = 0 := by
    change dualNumberAugmentation (ZMod 2)
      (DualNumber.eps : DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2))) = 0
    refine Prod.ext ?_ ?_ <;> simp
  rw [one_tmul_smul_eq_algebraMap_tmul, hyφ, TensorProduct.zero_tmul]

/-- An element of a projective module killed by reduction modulo the augmentation lies in
`(x² - x) M + ε M`. -/
theorem exists_eq_relation_smul_add_eps_smul (M : Type) [AddCommGroup M]
    [Module (MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))) M]
    [Module.Projective (MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))) M] (z : M)
    (hz : (1 : ZMod 2 × ZMod 2) ⊗ₜ[MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))] z = 0) :
    ∃ a b : M, z = MulOpposite.op (TrivSqZeroExt.inl (Polynomial.X ^ 2 - Polynomial.X) :
        DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2))) • a +
      MulOpposite.op (DualNumber.eps : DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2))) • b := by
  refine tensor_one_eq_zero_of_projective _ _ (fun a ha => ?_) z hz
  obtain ⟨b, c, hbc⟩ := dualNumberAugmentation_eq_zero_iff a.unop ha
  refine ⟨MulOpposite.op b, MulOpposite.op c, MulOpposite.unop_injective ?_⟩
  change a.unop = b * TrivSqZeroExt.inl (Polynomial.X ^ 2 - Polynomial.X) + c * DualNumber.eps
  simpa only [mul_comm] using hbc

/-- **The total homology of a finite graded-projective differential graded module over the
algebra of Tag 09R4 has even dimension.** Reducing modulo the ideal generated by `x² - x` and `y`
does not change the homology — the kernel of the reduction is acyclic, by
`Stacks.Examples.cycle_in_relation_ideal_is_boundary` — and the reduction is a finite complex of
free `k × k`-modules, so its dimension over `k`, hence the dimension of its homology, is even. -/
theorem even_finrank_totalHomology
    (P : DifferentialGradedModule.{0, 0, 0} charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra) (hfin : IsFinite P)
    (hproj : Stacks.DifferentialGraded.DGModule.IsProjectiveAsGradedModule.{0, 0, 0, 0}
      (Unbundled.module P)) :
    Even (Module.finrank (ZMod 2)
      (LinearMap.ker P.d ⧸ (LinearMap.range P.d).comap (LinearMap.ker P.d).subtype)) := by
  have : Module.Finite (MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))) P.M := hfin
  have : Module.Projective (MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))) P.M :=
    moduleProjective_of_isProjectiveAsGradedModule P hproj
  have : SMulCommClass (ZMod 2) (MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2))))
      (ZMod 2 × ZMod 2) :=
    ⟨fun k r b => smul_comm k (algebraMap (MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2))))
      (ZMod 2 × ZMod 2) r) b⟩
  let D : ((ZMod 2 × ZMod 2) ⊗[MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))] P.M) →ₗ[ZMod 2]
      ((ZMod 2 × ZMod 2) ⊗[MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))] P.M) :=
    (charTwoReducedDifferential P).restrictScalars (ZMod 2)
  let f : P.M →ₗ[ZMod 2]
      ((ZMod 2 × ZMod 2) ⊗[MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))] P.M) :=
    AddMonoidHom.toZModLinearMap 2
      ((TensorProduct.mk (MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))) (ZMod 2 × ZMod 2)
        P.M 1).toAddMonoidHom)
  let p₀ : MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2))) :=
    MulOpposite.op (TrivSqZeroExt.inl (Polynomial.X ^ 2 - Polynomial.X))
  let y₀ : MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2))) := MulOpposite.op DualNumber.eps
  let smulMap (a : MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))) : P.M →ₗ[ZMod 2] P.M :=
    AddMonoidHom.toZModLinearMap 2
      { toFun := fun z => a • z, map_zero' := smul_zero _, map_add' := smul_add _ }
  let pmap := smulMap p₀
  let ymap := smulMap y₀
  have hp : Function.Injective pmap := relation_smul_injective P.M
  have hy : ymap ∘ₗ ymap = 0 := by
    refine LinearMap.ext fun z => ?_
    change y₀ • (y₀ • z) = 0
    rw [← mul_smul, show y₀ * y₀ = 0 from MulOpposite.unop_injective DualNumber.eps_mul_eps]
    exact zero_smul (MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))) z
  have hs : Function.Surjective f := tensor_one_surjective_of_surjective fun z =>
    let ⟨a, ha⟩ := dualNumberAugmentation_surjective (ZMod 2) z
    ⟨MulOpposite.op a, ha⟩
  have hfd : ∀ z, f (P.d z) = D (f z) := fun z =>
    (charTwoReducedDifferential_tmul P 1 z).symm
  rw [(homologyEquivOfSurjectiveWithAcyclicKernel P.d D f P.d_comp_d hfd hs
    (cycle_in_relation_ideal_is_boundary P.d pmap ymap f P.d_comp_d hy
      (charTwo_d_eps_smul_add_eps_smul_d P) hp (tensor_one_eps_smul_eq_zero P.M)
      (exists_eq_relation_smul_add_eps_smul P.M))).finrank_eq]
  have hD : D ∘ₗ D = 0 :=
    LinearMap.ext fun z => LinearMap.congr_fun (charTwoReducedDifferential_comp_self P) z
  have : FiniteDimensional (ZMod 2)
      ((ZMod 2 × ZMod 2) ⊗[MulOpposite (DualNumber (Polynomial (ZMod 2)))] P.M) :=
    Module.Finite.trans (ZMod 2 × ZMod 2) _
  exact (even_finrank_homology_iff D hD).mpr (even_finrank_charTwoReduction P.M)

end Reduction

/-! ### The total homology of a module with cohomology only in degree zero -/

section TotalCohomology

variable {R : Type u} [CommRing R] {A : DifferentialGradedAlgebra.{u, u} R}

/-- The projection of a differential graded module onto its degree-`n` homogeneous component. -/
def gradingProjection (P : DifferentialGradedModule.{u, u, u} A) (n : ℤ) : P.M →ₗ[R] P.M :=
  (P.grading n).subtype ∘ₗ (DirectSum.component R ℤ (fun i => P.grading i) n) ∘ₗ
    (DirectSum.decomposeLinearEquiv P.grading).toLinearMap

/-- The degree-`n` projection lands in the degree-`n` component. -/
theorem gradingProjection_mem (P : DifferentialGradedModule.{u, u, u} A) (n : ℤ) (x : P.M) :
    gradingProjection P n x ∈ P.grading n := ((DirectSum.decompose P.grading x) n).property

/-- The degree-`n` projection fixes elements of degree `n`. -/
theorem gradingProjection_of_mem (P : DifferentialGradedModule.{u, u, u} A) (n : ℤ) {x : P.M}
    (hx : x ∈ P.grading n) : gradingProjection P n x = x :=
  DirectSum.decompose_of_mem_same P.grading hx

/-- The degree-`n` projection kills elements homogeneous of degree `i ≠ n`. -/
theorem gradingProjection_of_mem_ne (P : DifferentialGradedModule.{u, u, u} A) (n i : ℤ)
    {x : P.M} (hx : x ∈ P.grading i) (hi : i ≠ n) : gradingProjection P n x = 0 :=
  DirectSum.decompose_of_mem_ne P.grading hx hi

/-- The differential shifts the homogeneous projections by one. -/
theorem gradingProjection_d (P : DifferentialGradedModule.{u, u, u} A) (n : ℤ) (x : P.M) :
    P.d (gradingProjection P n x) = gradingProjection P (n + 1) (P.d x) := by
  induction x using DirectSum.Decomposition.inductionOn P.grading with
  | zero => simp only [map_zero]
  | add a b ha hb => simp only [map_add, ha, hb]
  | @homogeneous i x =>
    by_cases hi : i = n
    · subst i
      rw [gradingProjection_of_mem P n x.property,
        gradingProjection_of_mem P (n + 1) (P.d_mem n x x.property)]
    · rw [gradingProjection_of_mem_ne P n i x.property hi, map_zero,
        gradingProjection_of_mem_ne P (n + 1) (i + 1) (P.d_mem i x x.property) (by omega)]

/-- Membership in the degree-zero coboundaries, with the source degree written as `-1`. -/
theorem mem_gradedCoboundaries_zero (P : DifferentialGradedModule.{u, u, u} A)
    (x : P.gradedCocycles 0) :
    x ∈ P.gradedCoboundaries 0 ↔ ∃ y : P.grading (-1), P.d y = (x.1 : P.M) := by
  rw [P.gradedCoboundaries_eq_range (show (-1 : ℤ) + 1 = 0 by norm_num)]
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨y, rfl⟩
    exact ⟨y, rfl⟩
  · rintro ⟨y, hy⟩
    exact ⟨y, Subtype.ext (Subtype.ext hy)⟩

/-- A homogeneous cocycle in a degree with vanishing cohomology is a coboundary. -/
theorem mem_range_d_of_isZero_gradedCohomology (P : DifferentialGradedModule.{u, u, u} A) (n : ℤ)
    (h : IsZero ((gradedCohomologyFunctor A n).obj P)) (x : P.M) (hx : x ∈ P.grading n)
    (hd : P.d x = 0) : x ∈ LinearMap.range P.d := by
  have hc : (⟨x, hx⟩ : P.grading n) ∈ P.gradedCocycles n := Subtype.ext hd
  have hsub : Subsingleton (P.gradedCocycles n ⧸ P.gradedCoboundaries n) :=
    ModuleCat.subsingleton_of_isZero h
  have hz : (Submodule.Quotient.mk (⟨⟨x, hx⟩, hc⟩ : P.gradedCocycles n) :
      P.gradedCocycles n ⧸ P.gradedCoboundaries n) = 0 := Subsingleton.elim _ _
  obtain ⟨y, hy⟩ := (mem_gradedCoboundaries _).1 ((Submodule.Quotient.mk_eq_zero _).1 hz)
  exact ⟨y, hy⟩

/-- A cocycle of a module with cohomology only in degree zero differs from its degree-zero part
by a coboundary. -/
theorem sub_gradingProjection_mem_range (P : DifferentialGradedModule.{u, u, u} A)
    (h : ∀ n : ℤ, n ≠ 0 → IsZero ((gradedCohomologyFunctor A n).obj P)) (x : P.M)
    (hx : P.d x = 0) : x - gradingProjection P 0 x ∈ LinearMap.range P.d := by
  classical
  have hz (n : ℤ) : P.d (gradingProjection P n x) = 0 := by
    rw [gradingProjection_d, hx, map_zero]
  have hn (n : ℤ) : gradingProjection P n x -
      gradingProjection P 0 (gradingProjection P n x) ∈ LinearMap.range P.d := by
    by_cases hn : n = 0
    · subst n
      rw [gradingProjection_of_mem P 0 (gradingProjection_mem P 0 x), sub_self]
      exact (LinearMap.range P.d).zero_mem
    · rw [gradingProjection_of_mem_ne P 0 n (gradingProjection_mem P n x) hn, sub_zero]
      exact mem_range_d_of_isZero_gradedCohomology P n (h n hn) _
        (gradingProjection_mem P n x) (hz n)
  have hsum : ∑ n ∈ (DirectSum.decompose P.grading x).support,
      gradingProjection P n x = x := DirectSum.sum_support_decompose P.grading x
  have ht := (LinearMap.range P.d).sum_mem
    (fun n (_ : n ∈ (DirectSum.decompose P.grading x).support) => hn n)
  rwa [Finset.sum_sub_distrib, ← map_sum, hsum] at ht

/-- **For a differential graded module with cohomology only in degree zero, the homology of its
total differential is its degree-zero cohomology.** -/
def totalHomologyEquivGradedCohomology (P : DifferentialGradedModule.{u, u, u} A)
    (h : ∀ n : ℤ, n ≠ 0 → IsZero ((gradedCohomologyFunctor A n).obj P)) :
    (LinearMap.ker P.d ⧸ (LinearMap.range P.d).comap (LinearMap.ker P.d).subtype) ≃ₗ[R]
      (gradedCohomologyFunctor A 0).obj P := by
  let g : LinearMap.ker P.d →ₗ[R] P.gradedCocycles 0 :=
    { toFun := fun x => ⟨⟨gradingProjection P 0 x.1, gradingProjection_mem P 0 x.1⟩, by
        refine Subtype.ext ?_
        change P.d (gradingProjection P 0 x.1) = 0
        rw [gradingProjection_d, show P.d x.1 = 0 from x.2, map_zero]⟩
      map_add' := fun x y => Subtype.ext (Subtype.ext (map_add _ _ _))
      map_smul' := fun r x => Subtype.ext (Subtype.ext (map_smul _ _ _)) }
  let F : LinearMap.ker P.d →ₗ[R] (gradedCohomologyFunctor A 0).obj P :=
    (P.gradedCoboundaries 0).mkQ ∘ₗ g
  have hker : LinearMap.ker F = (LinearMap.range P.d).comap (LinearMap.ker P.d).subtype := by
    ext x
    rw [LinearMap.mem_ker, Submodule.mem_comap]
    change (P.gradedCoboundaries 0).mkQ (g x) = 0 ↔ (x : P.M) ∈ LinearMap.range P.d
    rw [Submodule.mkQ_apply, Submodule.Quotient.mk_eq_zero, mem_gradedCoboundaries_zero]
    constructor
    · rintro ⟨y, hy⟩
      have h₂ : gradingProjection P 0 x.1 ∈ LinearMap.range P.d := ⟨y.1, hy⟩
      simpa using (LinearMap.range P.d).add_mem (sub_gradingProjection_mem_range P h x.1 x.2) h₂
    · rintro ⟨y, hy⟩
      refine ⟨⟨gradingProjection P (-1) y, gradingProjection_mem P (-1) y⟩, ?_⟩
      change P.d (gradingProjection P (-1) y) = gradingProjection P 0 x.1
      rw [gradingProjection_d, show ((-1 : ℤ) + 1) = 0 by norm_num, hy]
  have hsurj : Function.Surjective F := by
    intro z
    induction z using Submodule.Quotient.induction_on with
    | H c =>
      have hd : P.d (c.1 : P.M) = 0 := congrArg Subtype.val (mem_gradedCocycles.1 c.2)
      refine ⟨⟨(c.1 : P.M), hd⟩, ?_⟩
      have hg : g ⟨(c.1 : P.M), hd⟩ = c :=
        Subtype.ext (Subtype.ext (gradingProjection_of_mem P 0 c.1.2))
      change (P.gradedCoboundaries 0).mkQ (g ⟨(c.1 : P.M), hd⟩) = Submodule.Quotient.mk c
      rw [hg]
      rfl
  exact (Submodule.quotEquivOfEq _ _ hker.symm).trans (F.quotKerEquivOfSurjective hsurj)

end TotalCohomology

/-! ### Tag 09R5 -/

/-- A retract of `K × K` on which the composite endomorphism is the second projection is
isomorphic to `K`. -/
def linearEquivOfSecondProjection {K V : Type*} [CommRing K] [AddCommGroup V] [Module K V]
    (i : V →ₗ[K] K × K) (r : (K × K) →ₗ[K] V) (hir : ∀ x, r (i x) = x)
    (hri : ∀ z, i (r z) = (0, z.2)) : V ≃ₗ[K] K := by
  refine LinearEquiv.ofLinearMap ((LinearMap.snd K K K).comp i)
    (r.comp (LinearMap.inr K K K)) (LinearMap.ext fun z => ?_) (LinearMap.ext fun x => ?_)
  · exact congrArg Prod.snd (hri (0, z))
  · change r (0, (i x).2) = x
    rw [← hri (i x), hir, hir]

/-- A retract of a two-dimensional space on which the composite endomorphism is a projection onto
one factor is one-dimensional. -/
theorem finrank_of_secondProjection_retract {K V W : Type*} [Field K] [AddCommGroup V]
    [Module K V] [AddCommGroup W] [Module K W] (e : W ≃ₗ[K] K × K) (i : V →ₗ[K] W)
    (r : W →ₗ[K] V) (hir : ∀ x, r (i x) = x) (hri : ∀ z, e (i (r z)) = (0, (e z).2)) :
    Module.finrank K V = 1 := by
  rw [(linearEquivOfSecondProjection (e.toLinearMap.comp i) (r.comp e.symm.toLinearMap)
    (fun x => by
      simpa only [LinearMap.comp_apply, LinearEquiv.coe_coe, LinearEquiv.symm_apply_apply]
        using hir x)
    (fun z => by simpa using hri (e.symm z))).finrank_eq, Module.finrank_self]

/-- Every nonzero cohomology group of a retract of the image of `charTwoRegularModule` in the
derived category vanishes. -/
theorem isZero_cohomology_of_retract_charTwoRegularModule
    {E : derivedCategory charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra}
    (i : E ⟶ (toDerivedCategory charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra).obj charTwoRegularModule)
    (r : (toDerivedCategory charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra).obj charTwoRegularModule ⟶ E)
    (hir : i ≫ r = 𝟙 E) (n : ℤ) (hn : n ≠ 0) :
    IsZero ((cohomologyFunctor charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra n).obj E) := by
  have hQ : IsZero ((cohomologyFunctor charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra n).obj
      ((toDerivedCategory charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra).obj charTwoRegularModule)) :=
    (isZero_gradedCohomology_charTwoRegularModule n hn).of_iso
      ((cohomologyFunctorIso charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra n).app charTwoRegularModule)
  refine (IsZero.iff_id_eq_zero _).mpr ?_
  rw [← (cohomologyFunctor charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra n).map_id, ← hir,
    CategoryTheory.Functor.map_comp,
    hQ.eq_of_tgt ((cohomologyFunctor charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra n).map i) 0, zero_comp]

/-- A retract of the image of `charTwoRegularModule` whose composite retraction is multiplication
by `x` has one-dimensional degree-zero cohomology. -/
theorem finrank_cohomology_zero_of_retract_charTwoRegularModule
    {E : derivedCategory charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra}
    (i : E ⟶ (toDerivedCategory charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra).obj charTwoRegularModule)
    (r : (toDerivedCategory charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra).obj charTwoRegularModule ⟶ E)
    (hir : i ≫ r = 𝟙 E)
    (hri : r ≫ i = (toDerivedCategory charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra).map
      (regularLeftMul _ Polynomial.X)) :
    Module.finrank (ZMod 2) ((cohomologyFunctor charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra 0).obj E) = 1 := by
  set H := cohomologyFunctor charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra 0 with hH
  set isoQ := (cohomologyFunctorIso charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra 0).app charTwoRegularModule
    with hisoQ
  have hid : (H.map i ≫ isoQ.hom) ≫ (isoQ.inv ≫ H.map r) = 𝟙 (H.obj E) := by
    simp only [Category.assoc, Iso.hom_inv_id_assoc, ← CategoryTheory.Functor.map_comp, hir,
      CategoryTheory.Functor.map_id]
  have hnat : H.map ((toDerivedCategory charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra).map
        (regularLeftMul _ Polynomial.X)) ≫ isoQ.hom =
      isoQ.hom ≫ (gradedCohomologyFunctor charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra 0).map
        (regularLeftMul _ Polynomial.X) :=
    (cohomologyFunctorIso charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra 0).hom.naturality
      (regularLeftMul _ Polynomial.X)
  have hkey : (isoQ.inv ≫ H.map r) ≫ (H.map i ≫ isoQ.hom) =
      (gradedCohomologyFunctor charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra 0).map
        (regularLeftMul _ Polynomial.X) := by
    calc (isoQ.inv ≫ H.map r) ≫ (H.map i ≫ isoQ.hom)
        = isoQ.inv ≫ (H.map r ≫ H.map i) ≫ isoQ.hom := by simp only [Category.assoc]
      _ = isoQ.inv ≫ (H.map ((toDerivedCategory charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra).map
            (regularLeftMul _ Polynomial.X)) ≫ isoQ.hom) := by
          rw [← CategoryTheory.Functor.map_comp, hri]
      _ = isoQ.inv ≫ isoQ.hom ≫ (gradedCohomologyFunctor charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra 0).map
            (regularLeftMul _ Polynomial.X) := by rw [hnat]
      _ = _ := Iso.inv_hom_id_assoc _ _
  refine finrank_of_secondProjection_retract charTwoRegularCohomologyEquiv
    (H.map i ≫ isoQ.hom).hom (isoQ.inv ≫ H.map r).hom (fun x => ?_) (fun z => ?_)
  · simpa using ConcreteCategory.congr_hom hid x
  · have hz : (H.map i ≫ isoQ.hom).hom ((isoQ.inv ≫ H.map r).hom z) =
        ((gradedCohomologyFunctor charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra 0).map
          (regularLeftMul _ Polynomial.X)).hom z := by
      simpa using ConcreteCategory.congr_hom hkey z
    rw [hz, charTwoRegularCohomologyEquiv_map_regularLeftMul]
    refine Prod.ext ?_ ?_ <;> simp

/-- **The obstruction behind [Stacks, Tag 09R5], with no compactness hypothesis.** A retract of
the image of the regular module of `A = k[x, y]/(y²)` whose composite retraction is multiplication
by `x` — that is, the object `Ker(x)` of [Stacks, Tag 09R4] — is not isomorphic in `D(A, d)` to
the image of any finite graded-projective differential graded `A`-module.

Being represented by `P` means that the image of `P` under the homotopy quotient followed by the
localization at the quasi-isomorphisms is isomorphic to `E`, which is the notion of condition (2)
of [Stacks, Tag 09RB]; finiteness is `DifferentialGradedModule.IsFinite` (Tag 09R1) and graded
projectivity is `Stacks.DifferentialGraded.DGModule.IsProjectiveAsGradedModule` (Tag 09K0), both
in the spelling used by the converse implication
`DifferentialGradedModule.isCompactObject_of_exists_finite_projective_representative`. What is
proved here is stronger than the source's claim in two ways: the hypothesis of Tag 09RB that the
morphisms out of `P` in `K(A, d)` already compute those in `D(A, d)` is not assumed, and
compactness of `E` is not assumed either.

If such a `P` existed it would have one-dimensional degree-zero cohomology and no cohomology
elsewhere, because `H^*(E)` is the image of the idempotent `(0, 1)` on `H^*(A) = k × k`; so the
homology of its total differential would be one-dimensional. But that homology is the homology of
the reduction of `P` modulo `(x² - x, y)`, a finite complex of free `k × k`-modules, whose
dimension over `k` is even.

Everything the argument consumes about `E` is in the four hypotheses below; compactness is never
used. That is what lets
`Stacks.Examples.not_forall_exists_finite_gradedProjective_of_retract_regularModule` avoid naming
any compactness predicate. -/
theorem not_representable_by_finite_gradedProjective_of_retract_charTwoRegularModule
    {E : derivedCategory charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra}
    (i : E ⟶ (toDerivedCategory charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra).obj charTwoRegularModule)
    (r : (toDerivedCategory charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra).obj charTwoRegularModule ⟶ E)
    (hir : i ≫ r = 𝟙 E)
    (hri : r ≫ i = (toDerivedCategory charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra).map
      (regularLeftMul _ Polynomial.X)) :
    ¬ ∃ P : DifferentialGradedModule.{0, 0, 0} charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra, IsFinite P ∧
        Stacks.DifferentialGraded.DGModule.IsProjectiveAsGradedModule.{0, 0, 0, 0}
          (Unbundled.module P) ∧
        Nonempty (E ≅ (toHomotopyCategory charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra ⋙
          (quasiIsomorphisms' charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra).Q).obj P) := by
  rintro ⟨P, hfin, hproj, ⟨e⟩⟩
  have eP (n : ℤ) : (cohomologyFunctor charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra n).obj E ≅
      (gradedCohomologyFunctor charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra n).obj P :=
    ((cohomologyFunctor charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra n).mapIso e).trans
      ((cohomologyFunctorIso charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra n).app P)
  have hzero : ∀ n : ℤ, n ≠ 0 →
      IsZero ((gradedCohomologyFunctor charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra n).obj P) := fun n hn =>
    (isZero_cohomology_of_retract_charTwoRegularModule i r hir n hn).of_iso (eP n).symm
  have hdim : Module.finrank (ZMod 2)
      ((gradedCohomologyFunctor charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra 0).obj P) = 1 :=
    (eP 0).toLinearEquiv.finrank_eq.symm.trans
      (finrank_cohomology_zero_of_retract_charTwoRegularModule i r hir hri)
  have heven := even_finrank_totalHomology P hfin hproj
  rw [(totalHomologyEquivGradedCohomology P hzero).finrank_eq, hdim] at heven
  exact Nat.not_even_one heven

/-- **[Stacks, Tag 09R5].** *"There exists a differential graded algebra `(A, d)` and a compact
object `E` of `D(A, d)` such that `E` cannot be represented by a finite and graded projective
differential graded `A`-module."*

The algebra is `A = k[x, y]/(y²)` over `k = ZMod 2`, with `x` of degree `0`, `y` of degree `-1`
and `d y = x² + x`, and `E` is the summand of the regular module cut out by the idempotent given
by multiplication by `x` (Tag 09R4). The obstruction is
`Stacks.Examples.not_representable_by_finite_gradedProjective_of_retract_charTwoRegularModule`;
compactness of `E` enters only to discharge the first conjunct. -/
@[stacks 09R5]
theorem exists_isCompactObject_not_representable_by_finite_gradedProjective :
    ∃ (A : DifferentialGradedAlgebra.{0, 0} (ZMod 2)) (E : derivedCategory' A),
      IsCompactObject.{0} E ∧
        ¬ ∃ P : DifferentialGradedModule.{0, 0, 0} A, IsFinite P ∧
            Stacks.DifferentialGraded.DGModule.IsProjectiveAsGradedModule.{0, 0, 0, 0}
              (Unbundled.module P) ∧
            Nonempty (E ≅ (toHomotopyCategory A ⋙ (quasiIsomorphisms' A).Q).obj P) := by
  obtain ⟨E, i, r, hE, hir, hri⟩ := exists_isCompactObject_retract_charTwoRegularModule
  exact ⟨charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra, E, hE,
    not_representable_by_finite_gradedProjective_of_retract_charTwoRegularModule i r hir hri⟩

/-! ### Tag 09RB's implication (1) ⟹ (2) fails on a retract of the regular module -/

/-- **The algebra of [Stacks, Tag 09R4] is bounded**: `Aⁿ = 0` as soon as `2 ≤ |n|`, because it is
concentrated in degrees `0` and `-1`. So it satisfies the hypothesis "`Aⁿ = 0` for `|n| ≫ 0`" of
[Stacks, Tag 09RB], with `c = 2`. -/
theorem charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra_grading_eq_bot {n : ℤ} (hn : 2 ≤ |n|) :
    charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra.grading n = ⊥ := by
  have h0 : n ≠ 0 := by rintro rfl; simp at hn
  have h1 : n ≠ -1 := by rintro rfl; norm_num at hn
  refine (Submodule.eq_bot_iff _).mpr fun x hx => ?_
  have hx' : x ∈ dualNumberGrading (ZMod 2) (Polynomial (ZMod 2)) n := hx
  rw [mem_dualNumberGrading] at hx'
  exact Prod.ext (hx'.1 h0) (hx'.2 h1)

/-- **A bounded differential graded algebra carrying a retract of its own regular module that is
not representable by a finite graded-projective differential graded module.**

*This is a statement about the Stacks Project, not a formalization of one of its tags.* The
implication (1) ⟹ (2) of [Stacks, Tag 09RB], read literally, is **a mistake in the Stacks
Project, pending review**: it is **contradicted by the source's own [Stacks, Tag 09R5]**. What
the source's proof does establish is registered under the tag, as
`DifferentialGradedModule.exists_retract_isFiniteFreeAsGradedModule_of_isCompactObject`: a
compact object is a *direct summand* of (the image of) a module which is *finite free* as a
graded module and satisfies `Hom_{K(A, d)} = Hom_{D(A, d)}`. The witness below is consistent with
that corrected form and contradicts only the literal one.

This statement is the machine-checked half of the argument. The other half is on paper, and is
the source's own:

* §09R4 says of `A = k[x, y]/(y²)` that "it is clear that `A` is a compact object of `D(A, d)`";
* it exhibits `A = Ker(x) ⊕ Im(1 - x)` in `K(A, d)`, so `E = Ker(x)` is a direct summand of `A`,
  and concludes "`Ker(x)` is a compact object as follows from [Stacks, Tag 09QH]";
* that algebra is concentrated in degrees `0` and `-1`
  (`Stacks.Examples.charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra_grading_eq_bot`), so it satisfies Tag 09RB's
  hypothesis "`Aⁿ = 0` for `|n| ≫ 0`" with `c = 2`;
* and [Stacks, Tag 09R5] says `E` has no finite graded-projective model.

Tag 09RB's implication (1) ⟹ (2) applied to that bounded `A` and that compact `E` contradicts the
last point. The statement refuted below is the specialisation of (1) ⟹ (2) along the first two
points: its hypothesis is that `E` is a *retract of the image of the regular module*, which the
source's own reasoning says implies compactness.

**Why the hypothesis is universe-free, and what that buys.** `IsCompactObject.{w}`
(`CategoryTheory/Triangulated/CompactObjects.lean`) tests the canonical map
`⨁ᵢ Hom(E, Fᵢ) → Hom(E, ∐ᵢ Fᵢ)` only on families indexed by `I : Type w`, whereas the source
quantifies over arbitrary index sets. Source compactness therefore *implies*
`IsCompactObject.{0}`, and we have **not** proved the converse; so refuting
`IsCompactObject.{0} E → (2)` would refute something formally stronger than the source's claim
and would settle nothing about the source. No compactness predicate occurs in the statement
below. Being a retract of `Q(A)` is a universe-free condition, and it implies compactness under
every reading of the word — summands of compact objects are compact (Tag 09QH), and
`Hom_{D(A, d)}(A, M) = H⁰(M)` commutes with direct sums of any size — so Tag 09RB's (1) ⟹ (2)
implies the displayed statement whatever the intended quantifier on index sets. That is the whole
point of stating it this way.

**Residual.** The equivalence of `IsCompactObject.{0}` with the source's compactness is still
unproved here, and nothing below depends on it. What remains on paper is only the source's own
step "a direct summand of `A` is compact", quoted above. -/
theorem not_forall_exists_finite_gradedProjective_of_retract_regularModule :
    ¬ ∀ (A : DifferentialGradedAlgebra.{0, 0} (ZMod 2)) (c : ℤ),
        (∀ n : ℤ, c ≤ |n| → A.grading n = ⊥) →
        ∀ (E : derivedCategory' A)
          (i : E ⟶ (toHomotopyCategory A ⋙ (quasiIsomorphisms' A).Q).obj (regularModule A))
          (r : (toHomotopyCategory A ⋙ (quasiIsomorphisms' A).Q).obj (regularModule A) ⟶ E),
          i ≫ r = 𝟙 E →
          ∃ P : DifferentialGradedModule.{0, 0, 0} A, IsFinite P ∧
            Stacks.DifferentialGraded.DGModule.IsProjectiveAsGradedModule.{0, 0, 0, 0}
              (Unbundled.module P) ∧
            Nonempty (E ≅ (toHomotopyCategory A ⋙ (quasiIsomorphisms' A).Q).obj P) := by
  intro h
  obtain ⟨E, i, r, _, hir, hri⟩ := exists_isCompactObject_retract_charTwoRegularModule
  exact not_representable_by_finite_gradedProjective_of_retract_charTwoRegularModule i r hir hri
    (h charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra 2
      (fun _ hn => charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra_grading_eq_bot hn) E i r hir)

/-- **Over a bounded differential graded algebra, a compact object of `D(A, d)` need not be
representable by a finite graded-projective differential graded module** — at whatever universe
the index sets of the direct sums testing compactness are taken.

*This is a statement about the Stacks Project, not a formalization of one of its tags.* It is the
negation of the implication (1) ⟹ (2) of [Stacks, Tag 09RB], read literally and with hypothesis
(1) as `CategoryTheory.IsCompactObject.{w}` for an arbitrary universe `w`, which is the shape of
the source's own hypothesis. That implication is **a mistake in the Stacks Project, pending
review**: it is **contradicted by the source's own [Stacks, Tag 09R5]**, and the contradiction is
machine-checked here. What the source's proof does establish is registered under the tag, as
`DifferentialGradedModule.exists_retract_isFiniteFreeAsGradedModule_of_isCompactObject`: a
compact object is a *direct summand* in `D(A, d)` of (the image of) a differential graded module
which is *finite free* as a graded module and satisfies `Hom_{K(A, d)} = Hom_{D(A, d)}`, with no
boundedness hypothesis. The two differences from the literal statement — direct summand rather
than representative, finite free rather than finite projective — are what the source's own
argument delivers, and the witness below is consistent with both: `E = Ker(x)` is a direct
summand of the regular module, which is finite free. What it contradicts is only the literal
reading.

The whole chain is now machine-checked:

* the algebra `A = k[x, y]/(y²)` of §09R4 over `k = ZMod 2`, with `x` of degree `0`, `y` of degree
  `-1` and `d y = x² + x`, is concentrated in degrees `0` and `-1`
  (`Stacks.Examples.charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra_grading_eq_bot`), so it satisfies Tag 09RB's
  hypothesis "`Aⁿ = 0` for `|n| ≫ 0`" with `c = 2`;
* §09R4's "it is clear that `A` is a compact object of `D(A, d)`" is
  `DifferentialGradedModule.isCompactObject_q_regularModule_arbitraryUniverse`, which holds at
  every index universe;
* §09R4 exhibits `A = Ker(x) ⊕ Im(1 - x)` in `K(A, d)`, so `E = Ker(x)` is a retract of `A`
  (`Stacks.Examples.exists_isCompactObject_retract_charTwoRegularModule`), and concludes
  "`Ker(x)` is a compact object as follows from [Stacks, Tag 09QH]"; that step is
  `DifferentialGradedModule.isCompactObject_of_retract_q_regularModule`, again at every index
  universe;
* and `E` has no finite graded-projective model, which is
  `Stacks.Examples.not_representable_by_finite_gradedProjective_of_retract_charTwoRegularModule`
  and, with the compactness, [Stacks, Tag 09R5].

**The universe caveat is discharged.** The statement
`Stacks.Examples.not_forall_exists_finite_gradedProjective_of_retract_regularModule` above takes
bare retract data in its hypothesis because, when it was proved, source compactness was known to
*imply* `CategoryTheory.IsCompactObject.{0}` and the converse was not available: refuting
`IsCompactObject.{0} E → (2)` alone would have refuted something formally stronger than the
source's claim. That is no longer the situation. The universe `w` below is a parameter of this
theorem, so the implication (1) ⟹ (2) is refuted *at every reading of the quantifier on index
sets* simultaneously, and the witness `E` is compact at all of them at once — compactness at an
arbitrary universe is exactly what
`DifferentialGradedModule.isCompactObject_of_retract_q_regularModule` provides. Nothing of the
source's compactness argument is left on paper.

Both refutations are kept: this one is the one shaped like the source's implication, and the
retract-data one has the stronger (universe-free) hypothesis, so it refutes a formally stronger
statement. Neither carries a Stacks tag; see the implementation notes of this file. -/
theorem not_forall_exists_finite_gradedProjective_of_isCompactObject :
    ¬ ∀ (A : DifferentialGradedAlgebra.{0, 0} (ZMod 2)) (c : ℤ),
        (∀ n : ℤ, c ≤ |n| → A.grading n = ⊥) →
        ∀ E : derivedCategory' A, IsCompactObject.{w} E →
          ∃ P : DifferentialGradedModule.{0, 0, 0} A, IsFinite P ∧
            Stacks.DifferentialGraded.DGModule.IsProjectiveAsGradedModule.{0, 0, 0, 0}
              (Unbundled.module P) ∧
            Nonempty (E ≅ (toHomotopyCategory A ⋙ (quasiIsomorphisms' A).Q).obj P) := by
  intro h
  obtain ⟨E, i, r, _, hir, hri⟩ := exists_isCompactObject_retract_charTwoRegularModule
  exact not_representable_by_finite_gradedProjective_of_retract_charTwoRegularModule i r hir hri
    (h charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra 2
      (fun _ hn => charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra_grading_eq_bot hn) E
      (isCompactObject_of_retract_q_regularModule charTwoDualNumberDGAlgebra i r hir))

end Stacks.Examples

